#### Problem 8: Migrating from Hive Partitioning to Liquid Clustering

**Scenario you describe:**
> "Our 10 TB events table was partitioned by `(year, month, day, hour)`. It had 850,000 partitions and 4.2 million files. Queries that didn't filter on date were extremely slow (full scan). OPTIMIZE took 12 hours. The metastore was buckling under partition metadata."

**Solution:**

| Phase | Action | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Analysis | Profiled 90 days of query patterns | 60% filtered by date, 25% by `event_type`, 15% by `user_id` |
| 2. New table | `CREATE TABLE events_v2 CLUSTER BY (event_date, event_type, user_id)` | Liquid clustering on the top 3 filter columns |
| 3. Migration | `INSERT INTO events_v2 SELECT * FROM events_v1` | Ran in batches by month to avoid timeout |
| 4. OPTIMIZE | `OPTIMIZE events_v2` | Compacted into \~1 GB files with Hilbert-curve layout |
| 5. Cutover | Renamed tables: `ALTER TABLE events_v1 RENAME TO events_v1_archive` then `ALTER TABLE events_v2 RENAME TO events` |
| 6. Validation | Compared query performance on 20 representative queries | Avg 3.2x faster, worst case (date-only filter) same, best case (multi-column filter) 8x faster |

**Before vs After:**

| Metric | Hive Partitioned | Liquid Clustered |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Partitions | 850,000 | 0 (no physical partitions) |
| Files | 4,200,000 | 12,500 |
| Avg file size | 2.5 MB | 850 MB |
| OPTIMIZE time | 12 hours | 25 minutes |
| Date-only query | 8 sec | 9 sec (comparable) |
| Multi-column query | 180 sec (full scan) | 22 sec (clustering prunes files) |
| Metastore overhead | High (850K partitions to list) | Minimal |

**What makes this principal-level:**
- You ran a **query pattern analysis** before choosing clustering columns — data-driven, not guesswork
- You designed a **zero-downtime migration** with table rename + archive
- You set up **ongoing OPTIMIZE scheduling** — liquid clustering improves with each OPTIMIZE as new data arrives

