#### Problem 11: External Tables for Multi-Cloud Compliance

**Scenario you describe:**
> "Regulatory requirement: raw PII data must remain in the EU region (Azure West Europe). But our analytics platform is in Azure Australia East. The compliance team mandated data sovereignty — PII cannot be copied to the AU region."

**Solution — External Tables + Views:**

| Layer | Location | Table Type | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Raw PII** | Azure West Europe (ADLS Gen2) | External Table | `CREATE TABLE eu_raw.customers LOCATION 'abfss://eu-container@eustorage.dfs.core.windows.net/customers'` |
| **Anonymized/aggregated** | Azure Australia East | Managed Table | PII stripped or hashed — safe to copy |
| **Cross-region view** | AU workspace | View over external table | `CREATE VIEW analytics.customers_safe AS SELECT hash(email) as email_hash, city, age_band FROM eu_raw.customers` |
| **Governance** | Unity Catalog | Column masks + row filters | `ALTER TABLE eu_raw.customers ALTER COLUMN email SET MASK mask_pii` |

**Tradeoffs communicated to stakeholders:**

| Aspect | External Table | Managed Table |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Query latency | Higher (cross-region network) | Lower (co-located) |
| Data sovereignty | Full control — data stays in EU | Data in Databricks-managed storage |
| DROP TABLE risk | Safe — only metadata deleted | Data deleted |
| Optimization | Must manually run OPTIMIZE on the external path | Auto-optimize built in |

**What makes this principal-level:**
- You designed a **hybrid architecture** that satisfies compliance without sacrificing analytics capability
- You used Unity Catalog **column masking and row-level security** to enforce PII protection at the platform level, not application level
- You documented the **latency tradeoff** (cross-region reads are 2-5x slower) and mitigated it with aggressive caching and pre-aggregation

