#### Problem 12: Implementing a Self-Healing Pipeline

**Scenario you describe:**
> "We had 120 ETL jobs. On average, 5-8 failed every night due to transient issues (network blips, spot instance preemption, source system timeouts). An on-call engineer spent 2 hours every morning manually retrying them. I eliminated the on-call toil entirely."

**Solution — Multi-Layer Resilience:**

| Layer | Mechanism | Handles |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Task-level retry** | Job config: `max_retries: 3` with exponential backoff | Transient network errors, spot preemption |
| **Checkpoint recovery** | Delta + Structured Streaming checkpoints | Job restarts from last committed micro-batch, not from scratch |
| **Idempotent writes** | All writes use MERGE or `CREATE OR REPLACE` — never raw INSERT | Safe to retry without duplicates |
| **Circuit breaker** | Pre-flight check: if source row count < 50% of yesterday's, SKIP and alert | Prevents loading bad/partial source data |
| **Dead letter queue** | Bad records written to `_quarantine` table, not dropped | No silent data loss |
| **Dependency-aware retry** | If bronze fails, silver and gold for that entity don't attempt — retry bronze first | Prevents cascading failures |
| **Automated alerting** | After 3 retries fail, PagerDuty alert with error context + runbook link | Human intervention only when automation can't fix it |

**Result:**

| Metric | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Daily manual interventions | 5-8 | 0-1 |
| On-call morning time | 2 hours | 10 minutes (review dashboard) |
| Data freshness SLA misses | 3-4/month | 0-1/month |
| Mean time to recovery | 45 min (human) | 3 min (automatic retry) |

**What makes this principal-level:**
- You designed a **framework**, not a one-off fix — every new pipeline inherits resilience patterns
- You quantified the impact in **engineering hours saved** and **SLA improvement** — speaks to leadership
- You used Databricks features together: Lakeflow Jobs retries + Delta checkpointing + MERGE idempotency + Unity Catalog lineage for dependency tracking

